N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit, every bar six eighths: verse bar 1 two groups of three even eighths (E A B, c B A); bar 2 quarter-eighth, quarter and eighth rest (c B A); bar 3 even eighths rising to E5 (A B c, e d c); bar 4 B4 dotted quarter and dotted-quarter rest, a half cadence on E; bar 5 quarter-eighth then even eighths (e d, c B A); bar 6 G# A B then E5 quarter and eighth rest; bar 7 quarter-eighth then B G# E; bar 8 A4 dotted quarter and rest. Refrain bars 9-16 use quarter-eighth pairs and dotted quarters only, ending bars 10 and 14 on the V chord and bars 12 and 16 on I. Melody pitch set E-G#-A-B-C-D-E, range E4-E5, largest leap inside a phrase a fourth (B4-E5 in bar 6, E4-A4 into bar 8); the one fifth, A4 up to E5, crosses the rest from the verse into the refrain (bars 8-9). Lower part (refrain only) B3-C5, a third below the melody on most notes, a fourth, fifth or sixth at phrase ends; its largest leap is the sixth C4-A4 across the breath between bars 12 and 13. Form: verse-refrain AB, 8+8 bars. Originality check: the opening E-A-B-C rise, the refrain's falling E-E-D-C and the "one goes high, one goes low" contrary shape were compared with "María Landó" and with common children's and school songs; no shared hook was found; stepwise minor-key lines of this kind are common property.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 5 voices hold the lower part against the melody at dotted quarter = 60, whether the E5 peaks suit the class, how the page's ballad band sounds in 6/8 and whether the percussion reply fits the bar-4 and bar-8 rests can only be settled by a classroom trial. The originality check is one reader's; a second score reader should repeat it. An Afro-Peruvian music educator should judge whether the listening framing is accurate and respectful.
